---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ContactButton from '../components/ContactButton.astro';
import { SECURITY_ADVISORY_URL } from '../lib/site';
const repo = 'https://github.com/bakarrovenin/assay-pilot-001-evidence';
const checks = [
  ['check-01', 'A', 'The alert is closed.', 'Re-run the scanner. Does it still report the original finding? A quiet scanner alone does not establish that a patch works.'],
  ['check-02', 'B', 'The attacks are blocked.', 'Run the attack cases against the patched application. Any successful attack fails this check. An application that cannot run is insufficient evidence, not a pass.'],
  ['check-03', 'C', 'Normal inputs still work.', 'Compare ordinary inputs with responses captured from a known-correct reference. A patch that rejects valid inputs fails, even if it blocks every attack.'],
  ['check-04', 'D', 'The warning was not suppressed.', 'Inspect the patch for suppression. Record the outcome alongside the scanner and execution results. This does not prove the author’s intent.'],
];
---
<BaseLayout title="How it works" description="The four checks behind an Assay verdict, the current SQL scope, and the limits of the evidence." path="/methodology">
  <article class="reading-page shell">
    <header><p class="eyebrow">How it works · method v1</p><h1>A fix has to survive<br /><span>more than its own tests.</span></h1><p class="intro">Assay checks AI-written SQL injection patches against attack cases and normal inputs. You get a verdict tied to the cases that actually ran.</p></header>
    <section id="contract"><h2>Four checks. One recorded verdict.</h2><p>A patch can pass every test and leave the hole wide open.</p><p>Current scope: four seeded Python/SQLite SQL injection challenges. They cover quoted strings, numeric values, ORDER BY identifiers and second-order injection.</p><div class="method-checks">{checks.map(([id,letter,title,body]) => <section id={id}><span class="check-letter">{letter}</span><div><h3>{title}</h3><p>{body}</p></div></section>)}</div></section>
    <section id="artifact"><h2>What the verdict means</h2><dl class="verdict-definitions"><dt>VERIFIED</dt><dd>All four checks passed for the tested cases. It is a scoped result, not a guarantee that the application is secure.</dd><dt>NOT VERIFIED</dt><dd>At least one check failed. The record identifies the failure and the relevant inputs.</dd><dt>INSUFFICIENT EVIDENCE</dt><dd>The checks could not establish a result. This is not counted as a pass.</dd><dt>NO ALERT</dt><dd>The tool did not produce the relevant alert and patch. It is reported separately from scored patches.</dd></dl></section>
    <section id="index-rules"><h2>Three different kinds of evidence</h2><p><strong>Early demonstration:</strong> three constructed patches showed whether the checks could distinguish a correct fix from two inadequate ones. It was not a provider comparison.</p><p><strong>Blind pilot:</strong> the fixer received the vulnerable module and reported payload. Attack cases, normal inputs and the reference implementation were withheld. The dated provider results come from this experiment.</p><p><strong>Public benchmark:</strong> challenges and test material are published. They let people inspect and exercise the checks, but results are not equivalent to a blind evaluation.</p><p>The proposed Index would evaluate real historical vulnerabilities. The seeded pilot is not that Index. Rates use scored patches as the denominator; no-alert and insufficient-evidence records remain separate.</p><a class="text-link" href="/benchmark/leaderboard">Inspect the recorded results ↗</a></section>
    <section id="harness"><h2>How a run is contained</h2><p id="harness-scope">The scoring service applies a submitted patch in a Docker container with no network access, resource limits and a timeout. It records the four check results and removes the container after the run. Hosted scoring needs a separate deployed backend; the website alone does not execute patches.</p><p><a href={`${repo}/tree/main/service`}>Service and deployment instructions ↗</a></p></section>
    <section id="limitations"><h2>What this does not establish</h2><ul><li>A small seeded SQL corpus does not establish a provider’s overall security or reliability.</li><li>Scanner blind spots, incomplete test coverage and an incorrect reference baseline can affect results.</li><li>Published test material can be learned or fitted to. A public benchmark cannot be treated as unseen evidence.</li><li>Passing these checks does not establish safety for other languages, vulnerability classes or business logic.</li></ul><p id="assumptions">The method depends on an executable environment and a trustworthy reference. Where either fails, the result must say so.</p></section>
    <section id="disputes"><h2>Inspect it. Reproduce it. Challenge it.</h2><p>The harness, recorded results and reproduction instructions are linked from the <a href="/evidence">evidence page</a>. The reports retain known failures in the method and the corrections made to it. Vendor responses are published unedited.</p><p><a href={SECURITY_ADVISORY_URL}>Report a security bypass ↗</a></p></section>
    <details class="reference-links" id="terms"><summary>Technical reference and historical detail</summary><p id="method-version">The <a href="/research/methodology-v1">archived method reference</a> preserves the detailed schema, terminology, harness gates and earlier product proposals. The <a href="/research/whitepaper-v1">original whitepaper</a> describes the early demonstration. These are historical references, not additional claims about current availability.</p></details>
    <div class="reading-actions"><a class="btn btn-primary" href="/benchmark">Explore the benchmark ↗</a><ContactButton /></div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
